Supraventricular tachycardia may cause no symptoms, or only brief ones when it comes and goes in episodes (paroxysmal). When symptoms happen they are palpitations, lightheadedness, fatigue, dyspnea (shortness of breath), chest pain or vague chest discomfort, nausea and vomiting. Symptoms may be more pronounced in the elderly and in people with underlying structural heart disease.

It usually does not lead to syncope (fainting) or hemodynamic collapse unless the heart rate goes above 200, as can happen in:
- atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia (the accessory pathway kind);
- atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia;
- atrial fibrillation associated with an accessory pathway (pre-excited atrial fibrillation, for example in Wolff-Parkinson-White).

So the rate is the line to watch: below 200, syncope and collapse are unusual.

Wolff-Parkinson-White is the classic accessory pathway condition: an extra pathway, the bundle of Kent, lets the atria reach the ventricles early. That early arrival is called pre-excitation, and it shows on an electrocardiogram taken in normal sinus rhythm as:
- a short PR interval (under 0.12 seconds);
- a delta wave, a slurred upstroke of the QRS complex;
- a widened QRS.

So a delta wave with a short PR means an extra pathway with the potential to cause reentrant fast rhythms.

The catch: look for pre-excitation on a tracing in sinus rhythm. During orthodromic tachycardia, the more common kind, the QRS is narrow with no delta wave, so in that fast rhythm the sign is missing.
